Negative control for world-adopt.spec.ts (T4.3, AC2.5 — design §3.1 ruling 2 /
§7.1, the DISPOSE branch).

The adopt rule is "same key AND same scope". This mutant keeps the key half and
drops the scope half, so ANY second boot of the same scenario key adopts —
including one at a different actor or a different `.for()` context. Nothing
throws, the world answers every call, and the port it answers with belongs to a
scope the operator is not looking at: the track drives an INVISIBLE SECOND
INSTANCE while the rendered surface sits unmoved. That is the precise risk
design §7.1 names, and without this control it is unpinned — the client-emails page's 11th
scenario (`Staff acting for a client…`) declares exactly this mismatch.

The `isEqual` import goes with the conjunct so the mutated file is clean; the
mutation is one condition, not a rewrite.

Note the asymmetry with the sibling `.dispose-shared-cell` mutant: the Playwright
bridge lane IS a net for this branch (its scenario 11 boots a second, different
scope inside one page), but it is not a net for the adopt branch (`tasks.md`
K12). Both mutants exist because neither branch's net covers the other.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Reds: world-adopt.spec.ts — the dispose cases (booting a different actor, or a
different `.for()` context id, under the same key still fires the previous
cell's `destroy` and mints a port at the newly named scope).

